    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. Let me verify my understanding is complete by confirming all rejection paths are covered.\n\nThe investigation shows the implementation **already fully satisfies the specification**:\n\n**What I checked:**\n1. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** (the only source of 400/409/429 responses) builds every rejection response via `detailBuild(...)`, which produces a Spring `ProblemDetail` carrying all four required members:\n   - `type` \u2014 `problemDetail.setType(...)`\n   - `title` \u2014 `problemDetail.setTitle(...)`\n   - `status` \u2014 `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n   - `detail` \u2014 `problemDetail.setDetail(...)`\n2. Every handler returns `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. Spring's `AbstractMessageConverterMethodProcessor` detects the `ProblemDetail` body type and serializes it as **`application/problem+json`** \u2014 this is body-type driven, so it applies uniformly to the 400 (`MethodArgumentNotValid`, `RequiredFieldsMissing`, `InvalidTelephone/Email/Postcode`, `FutureRegistrationDate`), 409 (`DuplicateTelephone/Household/Email/Identity`, `CityAtCapacity`), and 429 (`DailyOwnerLimitExceeded`) handlers alike.\n3. I grepped all controllers/services for any hand-built 400/409/429 responses that could bypass the advice \u2014 there are none; controllers only emit `OK`/`CREATED`/`NOT_FOUND`/`NO_CONTENT`. So all rejections flow through the `ProblemDetail` advice.\n\n**Test results** (offline Maven, `./mvnw -o test`):\n```\nTests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0\nBUILD SUCCESS\n```\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` \u2014 which posts an owner missing `city`, then asserts `400`, `contentTypeCompatibleWith(\"application/problem+json\")`, and `$.status == 400` \u2014 passes, as does the whole suite.\n\nNo code changes were required: the application already returns RFC7807 `application/problem+json` bodies with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail` for all 400/409/429 rejections, with unchanged status codes. The suite is green.",
